Coffee is a big part of my mornings up here, and the single biggest upgrade I've made to it wasn't a new brewer or fancier beans. It was grinding my own beans right before I brew.

Ground coffee starts going stale fast. Most of the aroma and flavor you smell when you open a fresh bag is gone within a day or so of grinding. Whole beans hold onto it much longer, so grinding right before you brew gets you a noticeably better cup from the same beans.

There's a nice bonus for blood sugar, too. Black coffee has essentially no carbs. When the coffee tastes good on its own, I'm a lot less tempted to load it up with sugar or sweetened creamer.

## Why a hand grinder?

You can get a great grind from an electric grinder, but a hand grinder has a few things going for it:

- **Better grind for the money.** A good hand grinder costs a lot less than an electric grinder with the same burr quality.
- **Quiet.** No loud motor at 6am while everyone else is asleep.
- **No power needed.** It works at camp, in the car or when the power goes out, which happens more than I'd like in the mountains.
- **Small.** It takes up about as much counter space as a pepper mill.

The trade-off is a little work. Grinding a single cup takes anywhere from 30 seconds to a minute of cranking, depending on the grinder and how fine you go. For one or two cups, I don't mind it. If you're brewing a full pot for a crowd every morning, an electric grinder is probably the better call.

## Burrs, not blades

Whatever you buy, get a burr grinder. Blade grinders chop beans unevenly, leaving a mix of dust and big chunks. The dust over-extracts and tastes bitter, and the chunks under-extract and taste sour. A burr grinder crushes beans between two burrs set a fixed distance apart, so the grounds come out much more even.

Every grinder on this list uses a conical burr.

## The grinders I'd pick

### A first grinder

If you've never owned a burr grinder, start here.

- **[TIMEMORE Chestnut C2](/coffee/products/timemore-chestnut-c2-hand-grinder):** My go-to recommendation for a first hand grinder. It holds about 25g of beans and grinds consistently for the price.
- **[wonplug Dual Cutter](/coffee/products/wonplug-dual-cutter-hand-grinder):** The budget pick. A big step up from pre-ground or a blade grinder without spending much.
- **[KINGrinder K5](/coffee/products/kingrinder-k5-hand-grinder):** A full-size grinder with an external adjustment dial that gives you a lot for the money.

### For travel and camping

- **[TIMEMORE Nano](/coffee/products/timemore-nano-hand-grinder):** The handle folds down, so it packs small.
- **[1Zpresso Q2](/coffee/products/1zpresso-q2-hand-grinder):** Slim and light, and it fits inside an AeroPress.

### Everyday filter coffee

For pour over, French press and drip-style brewing every morning.

- **[1Zpresso JX](/coffee/products/1zpresso-jx-hand-grinder):** Full-size with a bigger burr, so it gets through a dose quickly.
- **[Normcore V2](/coffee/products/normcore-v2-hand-grinder):** A 38mm conical burr and a good all-rounder across brew methods.

### If you make espresso, or switch methods a lot

Espresso needs a very fine, very precise grind, and small changes make a big difference in the cup. These grinders have finer adjustment steps and an external adjustment ring that makes it easy to get back to a setting.

- **[1Zpresso J-Max](/coffee/products/1zpresso-j-max-hand-grinder):** Built for espresso first.
- **[1Zpresso K-Plus](/coffee/products/1zpresso-k-plus-hand-grinder):** Easy to switch between espresso and pour over.
- **[1Zpresso K-Max](/coffee/products/1zpresso-k-max-hand-grinder):** Near the top of the 1Zpresso lineup, and one you won't outgrow.

### The splurge

- **[Comandante C60 Nitro Blade](/coffee/products/comandante-c60-hand-grinder):** Nitrogen-hardened steel burrs and a build that lasts for years. A lot of coffee people end up here eventually.

## Getting started

A few tips once you have a grinder:

- **Weigh your beans.** A cheap kitchen scale makes your coffee a lot more consistent. A good starting point is about 1g of coffee for every 15-17g of water.
- **Grind right before you brew.** That's the whole point.
- **Start in the middle and adjust.** If the coffee tastes sour or weak, grind finer. If it tastes bitter or harsh, grind coarser.
- **Keep it clean.** Brush out the burrs every so often. Old coffee oils go rancid and can make fresh coffee taste stale.

